1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Hello everyone, in this tutorial video, we will learn how to use the historical record function.

2
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:23,000
First, in Marvelous Designer, if you want to go back to the previous operation, press Ctrl Z to go back to the previous operation.

3
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Press Ctrl Y to go back to the previous operation.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,000
If you want to quickly go back to the previous operation, you can click on the Library window and find the History window.

5
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
In the History window, you will see the previous operation steps.

6
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
If you click on one of the operation steps, you will return to this operation step.

7
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
The current operation steps will be the same as the previous operation steps.

8
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
The current operation steps will be displayed in orange.

9
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:06,000
If you are a virtual model who has changed the movement, you can only change the movement of the model.

10
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:13,000
You have to click on the next step to go back to the previous operation step.

11
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:20,000
If you have done other operations after going back to the previous operation step, all the previous operations will be covered.

12
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,000
For example, after the previous operation step,

13
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,000
you will see the operation steps that have been added.

14
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,000
But now, because we have selected the version, those operations have been covered.

15
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:36,000
If we want to save all the virtual models and costumes in the current 3D window,

16
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:43,000
we can click on the historical record 3D costume status in the upper right corner of the historical record.

17
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:48,000
When there is a 3D record in the 3D costume record box in the upper left corner,

18
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
it represents all the objects in the current 3D window.

19
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,000
All the objects in the current 3D window have been recorded.

20
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
It is worth noting that when we go back to the previous operation steps,

21
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
as long as we do not perform other operation steps, the later operation steps will not be covered.

22
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,000
For example, we go back to the previous operation steps and then click on the 3D costume record,

23
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,000
we can add a new 3D costume record.

24
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Because considering what I said before,

25
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
if we do other operations after going back to the operation steps,

26
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
then the later steps will be covered.

27
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:24,000
So we can save a 3D costume record in advance.

28
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:28,000
But when we open the 3D costume record,

29
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
it will prompt you to delete the current work record.

30
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:36,000
In other words, when you open a 3D costume record,

31
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
all the historical records we saved before will be deleted.

32
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
And when we double-click to open the 3D costume record,

33
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:49,000
we will see that it actually opens a temporary project file.

34
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Here we will see that when we double-click to open the 3D costume record,

35
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
the previous operation steps have been cleared.

36
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Because we have added a 3D costume record before,

37
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,000
we can double-click to open another costume record.

38
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
This is also the same as before.

39
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:07,000
Click to confirm.

40
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:14,000
After opening it, you will see that it has now switched to the costume wearing effect under the virtual model.

41
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
In fact, the 3D costume records we saved

42
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:23,000
are all some project files in the C drive.

43
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
If we want to find these two project files,

44
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
click on the file in the upper left corner to save as a project,

45
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
you can find these two files.

46
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:35,000
After finding them, we can copy the path in these two files,

47
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:38,000
and paste these two files.

48
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Because these two files are temporarily stored in the C drive,

49
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:46,000
so if you need them, just paste them.

50
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,000
So in essence,

51
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
the 3D costume record state is also a temporary way to save costume files.

52
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:59,000
That's it for this episode of the introduction of the 3D costume record function.

53
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:00,000
See you next time.

54
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Bye bye.

